    {
      "sample_id": "MAT212-534c009bcb33f62d",
      "question": "What is the fundamental procedure for deriving a Maclaurin series for a function, and to what broader class of problems does this repeated differentiation and evaluation at x = 0 apply?",
      "reference_answer": "The fundamental procedure is to repeatedly differentiate the function and evaluate the derivatives at x = 0 to determine the coefficients. As long as a function can be differentiated over and over again and the derivative values at x = 0 can be found, this method can be used to express any such function as a series of ascending powers of x.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source_id": "828e92d5-c69c-4d87-b0db-f3fbf971eae3",
          "chunk_id": "4f1c733f-2c0e-4a6e-8bbe-0bf2aa10a1d9",
          "quote": "As long as we can differentiate a given function over and over again, and find the values of the derivatives when we put x = 0, then we can use this method to express any function as a series of ascending powers of x.",
          "locator": "page 1",
          "evidence_group": null
        }
      ],
      "answerable": true,
      "question_type": "conceptual",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "split": "development",
      "authoring_method": "Kimi/OpenRouter source-bound draft",
      "integrity": "verified"
    },
    {
      "sample_id": "MAT212-fd3266f623ce5f68",
      "question": "Why are Lagrange multipliers considered advantageous over direct substitution in constrained optimization, and is it always necessary to solve for the multiplier λ explicitly?",
      "reference_answer": "Lagrange multipliers are advantageous because they avoid the need to rewrite the constraint g(x,y)=0 explicitly as y=h(x), which can be difficult or algebraically messy. Moreover, it is not always necessary to find the explicit value of λ to determine the solution to the problem.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source_id": "1ee86691-6c75-4c94-96c6-25551ad8ab0e",
          "chunk_id": "0b380e82-3b15-4356-828f-f5248b3ee68a",
          "quote": "Lagrange multipliers are particularly useful when it is difficult (or just algebraically messy!) to rewrite g(x,y) = 0 in the form y = h(x).",
          "locator": "page 13",
          "evidence_group": null
        },
        {
          "source_id": "1ee86691-6c75-4c94-96c6-25551ad8ab0e",
          "chunk_id": "0b380e82-3b15-4356-828f-f5248b3ee68a",
          "quote": "L(x,y,λ) = f(x,y)−λg(x,y).",
          "locator": "page 13",
          "evidence_group": null
        },
        {
          "source_id": "1ee86691-6c75-4c94-96c6-25551ad8ab0e",
          "chunk_id": "0b380e82-3b15-4356-828f-f5248b3ee68a",
          "quote": "Note that in Example 12 we did not need to find λ in order to find the solution to the problem.",
