    {
      "sample_id": "MAT212-fbf828d4e8de0aa1",
      "question": "How does Leibnitz’s rule transform a second-order linear differential equation into a recurrence for its Maclaurin coefficients, and why do only the values of y and its first derivative at x = 0 remain arbitrary?",
      "reference_answer": "Applying Leibnitz’s rule to the differential equation yields a relation among the higher-order derivatives; evaluated at x = 0 this produces a recurrence such as y^(n+2) = −(n+1)y^(n). Because the equation is second order, the values of y(0) and y'(0) supply the two arbitrary constants, and all higher derivatives are then fixed by the recurrence and the initial conditions.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source_id": "01dd362b-a1c7-4cda-b997-4d4f8745d66a",
          "chunk_id": "e3398986-1046-4fd8-aeb1-500acdcaee18",
          "quote": "y(n+2) +{xy(n+1) +n.1.y(n)}+y(n) = 0",
          "locator": "page 3",
          "evidence_group": null
        },
        {
          "source_id": "01dd362b-a1c7-4cda-b997-4d4f8745d66a",
          "chunk_id": "e3398986-1046-4fd8-aeb1-500acdcaee18",
          "quote": "∴ atx = 0 y(n+2) +(n+1)y(n) = 0 y(n+2) = −(n+1)y(n)",
          "locator": "page 3",
          "evidence_group": null
        },
        {
          "source_id": "01dd362b-a1c7-4cda-b997-4d4f8745d66a",
          "chunk_id": "e3398986-1046-4fd8-aeb1-500acdcaee18",
          "quote": "The values (y) and (y(cid:48)) provide the two arbitrary constants for the second 0 0\norder equation and are obtained from the given initial conditions.",
          "locator": "page 3",
          "evidence_group": null
        }
      ],
